WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.657
Hey, good morning, Ziad.

00:00:01.657 --> 00:00:04.005
Hope you slept off the storm okay.

00:00:04.005 --> 00:00:08.494
It's Friday, November twenty-seventh, the day after Thanksgiving.

00:00:08.494 --> 00:00:13.811
Calendar's got a short one today, done by one, with the whole afternoon open.

00:00:13.811 --> 00:00:16.643
Here's one to think about while you move.

00:00:16.643 --> 00:00:24.722
What's the name for the temperature where a plastic goes from stiff to kind of rubbery, way before it actually melts?

00:00:24.722 --> 00:00:26.103
Answer's at the end.

00:00:26.103 --> 00:00:27.070
Okay, weather.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:37.636
The storm's mostly done, but it stays breezy and gray with a stray shower or two, mid-fifties this morning and sliding into the upper forties by evening.

00:00:37.636 --> 00:00:42.194
Saturday's dry and windy, and the next rain shows up Sunday night.

00:00:42.194 --> 00:00:43.160
Okay, the day.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.268
Calendar has the gym at seven, straight sets.

00:00:46.268 --> 00:00:49.583
Then Godinger, but just a half day, nine to one.

00:00:49.583 --> 00:00:52.069
After that, nothing on there at all.

00:00:52.069 --> 00:00:55.314
A free Friday afternoon, which is kind of rare.

00:00:55.314 --> 00:00:56.281
Alright, news.

00:00:56.281 --> 00:00:57.317
Closer to home.

00:00:57.317 --> 00:01:01.253
The storm actually pushed the Macy's Thanksgiving parade.

00:01:01.253 --> 00:01:09.057
For the first time in its hundred years, it got moved, from Thursday to Saturday, because of gusts up near sixty.

00:01:09.057 --> 00:01:16.101
So if you're anywhere near Manhattan tomorrow morning, expect the usual parade chaos, just a day late.

00:01:16.101 --> 00:01:17.551
One from your corner.

00:01:17.551 --> 00:01:24.871
OpenAI is merging the usage limits for ChatGPT's regular chat and its Work mode into one shared allowance.

00:01:24.871 --> 00:01:28.600
So if you lean on both, you'll hit the ceiling sooner.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.467
The faster, lighter model still works after you run out.

00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:36.196
It's starting small and rolling out wider in December.

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:37.784
And an update on Congo.

00:01:37.784 --> 00:01:46.555
The Ebola outbreak in the east has now passed twelve thousand confirmed cases, which makes it one of the biggest ever recorded.

00:01:46.555 --> 00:01:51.043
It's still mostly contained to that region, but it keeps growing.

00:01:51.043 --> 00:01:53.944
Okay, here's one for two Sundays from now.

00:01:53.944 --> 00:02:01.195
On Sunday, December thirteenth, there's a Bushwick Holiday Makers Market on Forrest Street, one to seven.

00:02:01.195 --> 00:02:08.169
It's small, about fifteen local makers, so jewelry, ceramics, knits, collage, even tarot, with a D.J.

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:10.034
and some treats and drinks.

00:02:10.034 --> 00:02:15.697
It's the kind of market where the person who made the thing is the one selling it.

00:02:15.697 --> 00:02:19.149
No price listed, so it's probably free to walk in.

00:02:19.149 --> 00:02:21.566
Today's challenge is for your body.

00:02:21.566 --> 00:02:29.025
Since the afternoon's open, take a real walk after work, at least half an hour, no destination, no shopping.

00:02:29.025 --> 00:02:33.030
Black Friday crowds are everywhere, so pick quiet streets.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:39.383
Keep the phone in your pocket the whole time and just let your legs loosen up after the gym.

00:02:39.383 --> 00:02:41.938
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:02:41.938 --> 00:02:44.839
Remember selling off that old camera gear?

00:02:44.839 --> 00:02:49.051
Here's the clever bit of economics hiding behind an eBay bid.

00:02:49.051 --> 00:02:54.092
Start with a normal auction, where the highest bidder pays what they bid.

00:02:54.092 --> 00:02:57.614
That sounds fair, but it makes everyone play games.

00:02:57.614 --> 00:03:02.517
If a lens is worth four hundred to you, you shouldn't bid four hundred.

00:03:02.517 --> 00:03:06.522
You'd shave it, guessing how low you can go and still win.

00:03:06.522 --> 00:03:07.904
Everyone's bluffing.

00:03:07.904 --> 00:03:11.425
Then there's a twist called a second-price auction.

00:03:11.425 --> 00:03:16.881
The highest bidder still wins, but they pay what the second-highest person bid.

00:03:16.881 --> 00:03:23.925
An economist named William Vickrey worked out why that's so neat, and it helped win him a Nobel Prize.

00:03:23.925 --> 00:03:29.933
In that setup, the smartest move is just to bid exactly what the thing is worth to you.

00:03:29.933 --> 00:03:35.595
Bidding lower doesn't save you anything, since your bid never sets your own price.

00:03:35.595 --> 00:03:39.048
It only risks losing to someone you'd have beaten.

00:03:39.048 --> 00:03:41.879
And bidding higher only risks overpaying.

00:03:41.879 --> 00:03:44.435
So honesty becomes the best strategy.

00:03:44.435 --> 00:03:47.956
eBay works almost like that, through proxy bidding.

00:03:47.956 --> 00:03:54.448
You type in your max, and eBay quietly bids for you, just one small step above whoever's next.

00:03:54.448 --> 00:04:00.939
So the winner usually pays about the second-highest max plus a little bump, not their own max.

00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:02.113
Here's the catch.

00:04:02.113 --> 00:04:03.978
Most people don't trust it.

00:04:03.978 --> 00:04:08.604
They wait and bid in the last few seconds, which is called sniping.

00:04:08.604 --> 00:04:12.748
Partly it's to avoid a bidding war with someone who'd react.

00:04:12.748 --> 00:04:19.101
And partly it's to dodge people who bid a little, then creep up and up as they get attached.

00:04:19.101 --> 00:04:22.761
So for you as a seller, the takeaway is kind of nice.

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:27.802
Your price is set by the second most eager buyer, not the most eager one.

00:04:27.802 --> 00:04:31.946
Which is why good photos and a clear listing matter so much.

00:04:31.946 --> 00:04:35.606
You want two people who really want it, not just one.

00:04:35.606 --> 00:04:40.854
So: calendar says gym at seven and Godinger till one, then a free afternoon.

00:04:40.854 --> 00:04:42.995
Take that long phone-free walk.

00:04:42.995 --> 00:04:45.274
The answer: the glass transition.

00:04:45.274 --> 00:04:47.000
Have a good Friday, Ziad.
